  "result": "There's a particular pleasure in not knowing something\u2014not the anxious kind of ignorance that makes you feel small, but the generous kind that opens a door. The world is constantly handing us doors like this. How does a bird know to build a nest? What does the inside of a piano sound like if you crawl in? Why do certain words feel heavy in our mouths?\n\nChildren live in this state naturally, asking why until the adults around them grow tired. But somewhere along the way, many of us stop asking. We mistake knowing for safety.\n\nI think curiosity is a form of courage. It requires admitting that you don't have the answer yet, that you're willing to be a beginner. To stand before a vast subject\u2014astronomy, poetry, the history of your own family\u2014and say, \"Teach me,\" is a vulnerable thing. But it's also where the best living happens.\n\nThe beautiful thing is that learning doesn't have to be grand. You don't need a degree or a library. You can learn the name of one tree on your street. You can learn how your grandmother made her soup. You can learn to sit still for five minutes and notice your own breathing.\n\nIn a world that often feels overwhelming, curiosity is a quiet rebellion. It refuses to flatten everything into certainty. It says the world is still worth paying attention to. And it promises that if you lean in, even slightly, wonder will meet you there.",
